  0. Definitions.

  "This License" means version 1.0 of the Crab Noncommercial Source
License, including all of its conditions. It does not mean the original
license from which these terms were adapted.

  "Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds
of works, such as semiconductor masks.

  "The Program" means Crab and any other copyrightable material
expressly released under this License by a person entitled to license
it. A "licensor" is such a copyright holder or authorized licensor for
the material concerned. Each licensee is addressed as "you"; licensees
and recipients may be individuals or organizations.

  To "modify" a work means to copy from or adapt all or part of the work
in a fashion requiring copyright permission, other than making an exact
copy. The result is a "modified version" or a work "based on" that work.
A "covered work" is the unmodified Program or a work based on the Program,
subject to the third-party and aggregation rules in sections 5 and 7.

  To "propagate" a work means to do anything with it that, without
permission, would make you directly or secondarily liable for copyright
infringement under applicable law, except executing it on a computer or
modifying a private copy. It includes copying, distribution, and making
the work available to the public, with or without modification.

  To "convey" a work means any propagation enabling other parties to
make or receive copies. Merely interacting with a user over a computer
network without transferring a copy is not conveying; section 13 still
applies to remote interaction with a modified version.

  "Commercial Use" means using, copying, modifying, propagating, or
operating a covered work for direct or indirect commercial advantage or
monetary compensation. It includes selling copies or access; incorporating
the work into commercial products or services; operating it in business,
production, or revenue-generating activities, including internal business
use; advertising-supported operation; and providing paid development,
deployment, hosting, consulting, support, or similar services based on the
work. Free distribution and disclosure of source code do not, by
themselves, make a commercial activity noncommercial.

  "Noncommercial Purposes" means purposes that do not constitute
Commercial Use. Personal study, private use, experimentation, and hobby
activities qualify when they are not undertaken for commercial advantage
or compensation. Work performed as part of employment or business
operations is not personal noncommercial use merely because an individual
performs it.

  "Appropriate Legal Notices" means reasonably prominent notices
identifying the applicable copyright notices, explaining that the work
comes without warranty except as expressly stated, identifying this
License and its noncommercial and source-sharing conditions, and telling
users how to obtain the License and Corresponding Source. An interactive
interface may provide these notices through a conveniently accessible
menu item or other prominent feature.

  1. Source Code.

  The "source code" for a work means the preferred form of the work
for making modifications to it.  "Object code" means any non-source
form of a work.

  A "Standard Interface" means an interface that either is an official
standard defined by a recognized standards body, or, in the case of
interfaces specified for a particular programming language, one that
is widely used among developers working in that language.

  The "System Libraries" of an executable work include anything, other
than the work as a whole, that (a) is included in the normal form of
packaging a Major Component, but which is not part of that Major
Component, and (b) serves only to enable use of the work with that
Major Component, or to implement a Standard Interface for which an
implementation is available to the public in source code form.  A
"Major Component", in this context, means a major essential component
(kernel, window system, and so on) of the specific operating system
(if any) on which the executable work runs, or a compiler used to
produce the work, or an object code interpreter used to run it.

  The "Corresponding Source" for a work in object code form means all
the source code needed to generate, install, and (for an executable
work) run the object code and to modify the work, including scripts to
control those activities.  However, it does not include the work's
System Libraries, or general-purpose tools or generally available free
programs which are used unmodified in performing those activities but
which are not part of the work.  For example, Corresponding Source
includes interface definition files associated with source files for
the work, and the source code for shared libraries and dynamically
linked subprograms that the work is specifically designed to require,
such as by intimate data communication or control flow between those
subprograms and other parts of the work.

  The Corresponding Source need not include anything that users
can regenerate automatically from other parts of the Corresponding
Source.

  The Corresponding Source for a work in source code form is that
same work.

  Corresponding Source must be complete for the version conveyed,
including your modifications, build and installation scripts, project
files, configuration, and resources needed for those activities.
Obfuscated code, a patch without the complete source, or a link only to
an upstream version that lacks your modifications is not a substitute
for Corresponding Source. You need not disclose personal credentials or
private signing keys where recipients can build and install a modified
version without them; section 6 otherwise governs required Installation
Information.

